{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-74459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b}{35},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}{{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{49}-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{7})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}}{35}-\\frac{15}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}-15}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,b=15,$ and $a+b=23.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-74459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "13"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "23"] ["math" "27"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "23"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,-\\sqrt{14}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325846" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,+\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325854" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-74457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{84}{85}"] ["math" "-\\frac{94}{97}"] ["math" "\\frac{74}{85}"] ["math" "\\frac{84}{85}"] ["math" "\\frac{94}{97}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{84}{85}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{24}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{24}{25})-(\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{7}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{65}-\\frac{84}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{325}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-271612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{36}{325}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=$ | $=$ | $-\\sqrt{5}\\,-\\,$ {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})(-\\frac{2}{3})-(-\\frac{1}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{5}-4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325991" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-325991" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-f