ula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{12}{15})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{30}-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-74458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7+\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-74458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{10}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-271617" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-271617" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-141444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b\\sqrt{3}}{15},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-141444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})(-\\frac{3}{5})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{15}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}-3\\sqrt{3}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,b=3,$ and $a+b=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-141444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-141444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-141444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-141444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{159}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $325$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{159}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{159}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe’re given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13})+(\\frac{24}{25})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}}{325}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{10}}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}-24\\sqrt{10}}{325}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f87a51cdc541f44e757dea4807ca9db59719f7923d8aa2b2a9bd4a761f75917e"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "q-325997" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/state.json" "file_sha256" "cc45efbeb737136426d3908f7b5114b6363f01af42234497cded508d5f4fbf9f"} {"question" "e-9992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \n\\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/example-9992.json" "file_sha256" "1f173ca2eae94750a8ead8f56de5ea66b8015990bdb86cbb1bc7179133ee1780"} {"question" "e-9992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{9}^{2}}{{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{225}-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{225}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{75}+\\frac{36}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/example-9992.json" "file_sha256" "1f173ca2eae94750a8ead8f56de5ea66b8015990bdb86cbb1bc7179133ee1780"} {"question" "e-3524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/example-3524.json" "file_sha256" "2b17fb223bc8221291b5347c7b463068a3caa850d3bb1bd0bfbf106ad4bfc7c0"} {"question" "e-3524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042565/example-3524.json" "file_sha256" "2b17fb223bc8221291b5347c7b463068a3caa850d3bb1bd0bfbf106ad4bfc7c0"}] "usage" {"q-74459" [] "e-3524" [] "e-9992" [] "q-74457" []} "basis" 1558 "retractions" [[17592186184783 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}, \\sin v = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{4}{5}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\cos (u + v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{6} - b}{35}$,\ncalculate the value of $a + b$."] [17592186184783 :question/answer-fields 17592186183587] [17592186184782 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos u = \\frac{4}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{15}{17}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos (u - v)$."] [17592186184079 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$."] [17592186184079 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} v &= 1 - \\sin^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{9}{15})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{9^{2}}{15^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{225}{225} - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{144}{225}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{12}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u - v) &= \\cos u\\cos v + \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15}) + (\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{75} + \\frac{36}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{72}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186183997 :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right)$."] [17592186183997 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} u &= 1 - \\sin^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (-\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{4^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u + v) &= \\cos u\\cos v - \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17}) - (-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{45}{85} + \\frac{32}{85} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{13}{85}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186183582 17592186183583 17592186183584 17592186183585 17592186183586 17592186183588 17592186183589 17592186183590 17592186183591 17592186183592] "immutable_fields" [[17592186183581 :answer-field/id] [17592186183581 :answer-field/key] [17592186183581 :answer-field/type] [17592186183587 :answer-field/id] [17592186183587 :answer-field/key] [17592186183587 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
